#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198545 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a116e0d-3309-5148-a0ba-16993bc0deb7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Given a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d437d98e-f38c-56cc-823b-edbd67bfa3e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8y - 2x^{2}y - 2y^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194172} {:answer/id #uuid "f8a94499-6557-53bf-ad04-10413d0e4353" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6y - 2x^{2}y - 2y^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194173} {:answer/id #uuid "fe6a5a1b-b4e5-5940-9056-6f4928bf88a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x^{2}y + 2y^{3} - 8y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194174} {:answer/id #uuid "2381c9de-0b34-5622-8673-b8fc9212cbc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x^{2}y + 2y^{3} - 6y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194175} {:answer/id #uuid "49c952a8-5533-5893-a81b-1ebd0aae5f4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4y - 2x^{2}y - 2y^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d437d98e-f38c-56cc-823b-edbd67bfa3e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8y - 2x^{2}y - 2y^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194172} :answer-field/id #uuid "864f0ac6-6c3d-5f29-9316-b3701a590ed8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194171}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca709c99-e06d-5a3f-baf0-9520bf4a14bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-161830" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/assets/fd18335a2034613658c65186c4fc6e3e74067e730183d3e66fbacf8ab3601928.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,z,\\,-y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper paraboloid ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,R=-y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-2x)-(4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot (-2y)+(-y))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(7y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨0,z,-y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(7y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8y-2{x}^{2}y-2{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a116e0d-3309-5148-a0ba-16993bc0deb7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Given a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bb4018d-fb62-5078-abdb-144bf1cffc4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194178} {:answer/id #uuid "9d7beeef-e8c3-518a-9fc7-d7f1ca7fafa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}} - 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194179} {:answer/id #uuid "c6a78bcc-7ce0-59ce-9080-35c91fd63b10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x - \\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194180} {:answer/id #uuid "51a7dbed-2bc4-5f6e-bb63-cee5076198c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194181} {:answer/id #uuid "53bf65aa-e583-54ae-a143-7475684d2d43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}} - 4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51a7dbed-2bc4-5f6e-bb63-cee5076198c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194181} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e2731c8-e10c-5eb5-803a-a055c8238d8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "74d7a79e-d57c-5768-84fc-b3ae4dae67d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-161862" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/assets/e61f3d7c23ef7876bec2f11c93974551058bcd32f28745898604926d84dbcfee.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨y,\\,0,\\,2x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=0$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨y,0,2x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower cone ${S}_{2}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2)=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2)=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=y,\\,Q=0,\\,R=2x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-0\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-2x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a116e0d-3309-5148-a0ba-16993bc0deb7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Given a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a069c36-b5b0-52d6-809f-19d65bb38ead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xy-y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386240} {:answer/id #uuid "76433b57-2b2c-51bd-8789-cbff918f080c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386241} {:answer/id #uuid "d7690d3c-75eb-5fbd-92ed-d1158ea79456" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386242} {:answer/id #uuid "7203265a-4390-527f-b95e-1643e43c34b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386243} {:answer/id #uuid "4a4ad1e8-fb8d-5045-adb0-4a637a9753ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y-x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76433b57-2b2c-51bd-8789-cbff918f080c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386241} :answer-field/id #uuid "279c2708-6652-5ba9-a0ae-e0281f94d404" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "62cc77f5-d29c-5b66-aeee-e4c2bff38586" :question/math-academy-id "q-161899" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081085/assets/8a48be0b4c101babf157a05959e923576ad5614113b3eec9e93fe747d5dc3803.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨1,\\,x,\\,0⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So, \n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper hemisphere ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=1,\\,Q=x,\\,R=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-1\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})-x\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})+0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨1,x,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}0\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A+0 \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x+xy}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4d69d3c-3bb7-5e99-b606-1bbd538fa1c4" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed3b0302-28ab-5be2-86c7-e2840bfa6cf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386247} {:answer/id #uuid "b4eec892-0639-5e9d-ae67-55af7c915d07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y+\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386248} {:answer/id #uuid "1514334d-b962-51ff-bc04-fd2348c7a74a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386249} {:answer/id #uuid "8798600b-a935-5e72-af8a-7ef618e4933c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4y+\\frac{2y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386250} {:answer/id #uuid "5ce8bf71-8f80-5f4d-97e5-01363250f04d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-\\frac{2y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1514334d-b962-51ff-bc04-fd2348c7a74a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386249} :answer-field/id #uuid "8924d504-3edb-5802-9332-5109510777e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8fd3ad8-a86b-542f-ab4a-6226468a4f6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161903" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,-z,\\,2y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the cone $z=4-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and the plane $z=0.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=4-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=0$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper cone ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=-z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-4,\\,R=2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(0)\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})-(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-4)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,-1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨0,-z,2y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}(-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y-\\frac{4y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386252 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4d69d3c-3bb7-5e99-b606-1bbd538fa1c4" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9a1b88a-014d-541f-ba9a-48c84dbaf1ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386254} {:answer/id #uuid "d2d8e0a8-8a62-5921-a1dc-3804fb6ca864" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4xy-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386255} {:answer/id #uuid "0ba42783-544e-5b07-94f7-1a6b12ab1787" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4xy+2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386256} {:answer/id #uuid "2fdc2d1f-f3e4-5c82-81f2-99ded537b932" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy+2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386257} {:answer/id #uuid "14d84f94-039f-5b5f-9505-53de3f5d6e12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ba42783-544e-5b07-94f7-1a6b12ab1787" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4xy+2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386256} :answer-field/id #uuid "df3fc501-8aec-587f-8354-4407ad8c7b26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1c5fb359-1792-5bae-84b5-842bbac13d34" :question/math-academy-id "q-161915" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨y,\\,0,\\,-z⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the paraboloids $z=1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper paraboloid ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})=-2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=y,\\,Q=0,\\,R=-z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot (-2x)-(0)\\cdot (-2y)+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider the lower paraboloid ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4)=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4)=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=y,\\,Q=0,\\,R=-z=4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot (2x)-(0)\\cdot (2y)+4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-2xy+4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}(2xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4xy+2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-5\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
